<Layout title="Chinook Music Store — Genre Revenue Analysis">
  <article>
    <p class="back-top"><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}projects`}>← All projects</a></p>
    <h1>Chinook Music Store — Which Genres Are Worth Investing In?</h1>
    <p><em>An analysis of 25 genres, 3,503 tracks, and $2,328.60 in revenue — using SQL and Tableau.</em></p>

    <iframe
      src="https://public.tableau.com/views/ChinookGenreRevenueAnalysis/Dashboard1?:embed=y&:showVizHome=no&:tabs=no&:toolbar=yes&:display_count=no"
      width="100%"
      height="800"
      frameborder="0"
      allowfullscreen
    ></iframe>

    <h2>The question</h2>
    <p>A music store can't invest equally in every genre. Which ones deserve budget — and which are dead weight? I analyzed the Chinook store's catalog on two axes: <strong>volume</strong> (total revenue) and <strong>value</strong> (revenue per customer), and asked where the two disagreed.</p>

    <h2>What I found</h2>

    <h3>Revenue is dangerously concentrated.</h3>
    <p>The top 3 genres — Rock, Latin, and Metal — account for <strong>63%</strong> of all revenue. Just 6 of 25 genres drive <strong>81%</strong>. The store is a 6-genre business wearing a 25-genre catalog.</p>

    <h3>The biggest genre has no room to grow.</h3>
    <p>Rock generates 35.5% of revenue and earns $14.01 per customer — double the next genre. But every single one of the store's 59 customers already buys Rock. It's a floor, not an opportunity.</p>

    <h3>The hidden gems are non-music.</h3>
    <p>TV Shows, Drama, Sci Fi &amp; Fantasy, and Comedy generate small revenue but high value per customer — and the highest revenue per track in the catalog. Sci Fi &amp; Fantasy earns <strong>$1.53 per track</strong>, nearly 4× more efficient than Alternative ($0.35). These genres are under-invested.</p>

    <h3>60% of the catalog is dead weight.</h3>
    <p>15 of 25 genres sit in the low-volume, low-value quadrant. They take up catalog space and licensing budget while contributing little.</p>

    <h3>Loyalty was overstated — until I fixed the metric.</h3>
    <p>A naive repeat-purchase measure suggested 82% of customer-genre relationships were "loyal." Refining it to require purchases on <em>separate visits</em> dropped it to <strong>42%</strong>. Nearly half the apparent loyalty was just people buying multiple tracks in one trip.</p>

    <h2>The recommendation</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Expand the hidden gems</strong> — TV Shows, Drama, Sci Fi &amp; Fantasy, Comedy. High value, low volume, clear upside.</li>
      <li><strong>Maintain Rock, Latin, Metal, Alternative &amp; Punk</strong> — they carry the store, but don't expect growth from Rock.</li>
      <li><strong>Audit the Filler 15</strong> — review licensing costs against revenue per track and cut the worst performers.</li>
      <li><strong>Use the honest loyalty metric</strong> — separate visits, not tracks bought.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Method</h2>
    <p>Built 7 core SQL queries (joins, nested aggregation, window functions, <code>CASE</code> classification) to score each genre on revenue, per-customer value, repeat behavior, and catalog efficiency. Visualized in Tableau Public.</p>

    <p><strong>Caveats:</strong> Chinook is a synthetic dataset (trends are illustrative, not real-market). The 59-customer base is too small for reliable country-level comparisons. Revenue is measured as <code>unit_price × quantity</code> across all invoices.</p>

